ests  the demonstrations started in opposition  of a now canceled tax reform plan and  they've involved into a broader protest  against poverty excessive use of force  by the police and the  inequalities in the health and education  systems violent clashes have broken out  between police and protesters at least  24 people have died though that number  is disputed  by human rights groups who say the real  toll is higher  a woman from mali has given birth to  nine babies that's only  known to have happened twice before in  recorded history  [Applause]  the woman is 25 years old she was  expecting seven babies that's what the  ultrasound  showed but there were nine all of them  delivered by c-section  at a hospital in morocco doctors say  they weigh between 1.1 and 2.2 pounds  each and will be in incubators for some  time  but for now the girls and boys and their  mother are doing well  how one long promised icebreaker turned  into two  the politics of shipbuilding just ahead  there is news tonight about the  long-promised plan to build new ships in  canada  including a long overdue polar  icebreaker federal shipbuilding is  highly political  big promises are often made and big  contracts get doled out and  sometimes they even get snatched away  before anything is built  our chief political correspondent david  aiken has the details of what promises  were made today  david well donna at one time the federal  government was going to build just  one heavy icebreaker and with a price  tag of likely  two billion dollars or more that would  have been quite the reward for the  shipyard and region that won that  contract  but rather than create winners and  losers in what could be an election year  the federal liberals have decided to  build not one  but two heavy icebreakers one  at the c-span shipyard in north  vancouver  it's fantastic news super excited to  keep our members working  long-term employment and it's obviously  good for the lower mainland and the west  coast in general  the second at the davy shipyard in the  quebec city region  picking two winners to split  multi-billion dollar contracts  each with thousands of potential jobs  helps the trudeau government avoid  political headaches  in bc and quebec though the liberals  deny  politics played a role in the icebreaker  contracts  of course we all defend our different  industries our own provinces  but this is the responsible and  necessary decision that we had to make  because we need those two ships  this isn't politics this is about  actually giving the men and women of the  coast guard  the tools they need to do the job that  canadians expect them to do  the opposition says the decision to  build two in two shipyards  well that's pure politics i said okay we  will invent another one  we will make two icebreakers instead of  one and we will promise  quebec city that they will have it it's  another election announcement  david it's something that's geared  towards an election wherever that may be  now we're not going to see any of these  icebreakers until  2030 but if there's an election in 2021  you may not hear much about that donna  okay david aiken in ottawa thank you  newfoundland and labrador is in  desperate financial shape and today a  long-awaited report called  the big reset lays out the harsh  realities and calls  for higher taxes and spending cuts the  premier's economic recovery team  recommends deep spending cuts to health  care and education  privatizing some public agencies  increasing income tax  and creating a wealth and second home  tax the aim would be to return to a  balanced budget in six years  newfoundland and labrador has the  highest per capita expenditures and  net debt of any province in the country  it is at risk  of not being able to pay salaries or  operate hospitals  the economic recovery team is also  calling on the government to dismantle a  provincial energy corporation which  oversees the troubled muskrat falls  hydroelectric project  and with one of the largest public  sectors in canada it says the provincial  government needs to re-evaluate its  relationship  with unions i'm crystal gabanzing in  cardiff wales for the first time  16 and 17 year olds are casting ballots  how voters and candidates are reacting  to the historic change  the right to vote is an essential part  of democracy and there's long been  debate including here in canada  about lowering the voting age that's  already happened in wales and scotland  for the first time 16 and 17 year olds  are able to vote in welsh parliamentary  elections  and it's given thousands of young people  a direct say in their future  it's hoped it will also increase  political engagement  crystal gomencing explains  the senate country overlooks cardiff bay  and  change is on the horizon no one  anticipates a parliamentary  shake-up still this election is historic  voters as young as 16 are casting  ballots  it can be so frustrating that you're so  ignored  and you're so unable to have a say yeah  it breeds apathy  we know that the people that vote the  most are often  the oldest harvey jones is 22  he became a candidate in bridgend to  steer policies towards issues important  to his  generation educational supports housing  and mental health initiatives extending  the franchise to as many people as  possible  is a really sensible move particularly  when we have so many 16 17 year olds  working  paying taxes going into the military  it's only right that they do  have a voice wales followed scotland  in granting young people that voice the  electoral reform society  continues to lobby for the voting age to  be reduced  across the uk we've seen over the last  year young people protesting around  climate change around black lives matter  things like that relate massively to the  decisions made in our welsh parliament  and young people 16 17 year olds and  above should have their voices heard on  those issues  there is also a vote 16 campaign in  canada and it has some political support  in bc  what's happening here is being closely  watched because it could lead to a  new more inclusive political era does it  have an effect in which it changes the  tone the tenor  and more important the look of politics  so it isn't  as the old cliche goes dominated by male  pale and stale even if this one doesn't  necessarily show the uptick as quickly  it shouldn't all be put on that because  things take time  time for young voters to build new  habits and for  parties to engage with new voters  crystal gomencing global news cardiff  wales  and that is global national for this  thursday i'm donna friesen tonight's  your canada  is this abandoned schoolhouse near pence  saskatchewan  thanks for watching farah nasser will be  at the anchor desk tomorrow and i will  see you on saturday for the new reality  take care of yourselves bye-bye
 

